**EXERCISE 2.18.** What is the meaning in words of the approximable mapping $h : \mathbb{B} \to \mathbb{B}$, where

$$h(0x) = 00h(x), \text{ and}$$
$$h(1x) = 10h(x),$$

for all elements $x \in |\mathbb{B}|$? Is $h$ an isomorphism? Does there exist a map $k : \mathbb{B} \to \mathbb{B}$ where

$$k \circ h = I_{\mathbb{B}},$$

and is $k$ one-one?

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_218`).** The insert-zero map is not surjective on ideal elements, admits a left inverse under composition of approximable maps, and that left inverse is not injective.

*Proof.* Failure of surjectivity, the left-inverse identity, and failure of injectivity. ∎
